<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">after the Battle of Malplaquet, <a href="/years/1709">1709</a>; widely popular from <a href="/years/1780">1780</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre&quot; (&quot;Marlborough is going off to war&quot;), also known as &quot;Mort et convoi de l'invincible Malbrough&quot; (&quot;The death and burial of the invincible Marlborough&quot;), is a French folk song.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song is a burlesque lament on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough</a>, written on a false rumour of his death after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-malplaquet">Battle of Malplaquet</a> in 1709, the bloodiest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. Marlborough in fact lived until 1722. It tells how his wife, waiting for his return from the war, receives the news that her husband has died, how he was buried, and how a nightingale sang over his grave. For years it was known only by tradition, and it does not appear among the many anecdotal songs printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the middle of the 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Pierre Beaumarchais used the tune in his 1778 play The Marriage of Figaro for a despairing love song sung by Cherubino, and in 1780 Carl Stamitz ended a sonata for viola d'amore and viola with it while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. When Prince Louis-Joseph, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a>, was born in 1781, his wet nurse Geneviève Poitrine sang it while rocking his cradle. The queen took it up, the king and the court followed, and it was sung in the state apartments, kitchens and stables of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. Nicolas Dalayrac quoted it in the first act of his opera Renaud d'Ast in 1787.</p><p>From the court it passed to the tradespeople of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and on from town to town and country to country. It became as popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a> is said to have come to hate Marlborough because he heard the tune everywhere on his travels in France. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> nobility, the name became Mambrú and children sang it while playing hopscotch; Fernando Sor wrote variations on it for guitar in 1826, and from Spain it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The craze faded slowly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> is said to have hummed the tune while crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> in June 1812. In the United Kingdom the melody was sung by the mid-19th century with the words For He's a Jolly Good Fellow, which were familiar in America by 1862, and it also became the tune of The Bear Went Over the Mountain. Henry Wadsworth Longfellow published an English version, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven">Ludwig van Beethoven</a> used the tune for the French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>'s Victory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
